---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="How fast-xml-parser Got OIDC Provenance in 12 Hours &mdash; Commit"
  description="Behavioral scoring flagged fast-xml-parser as CRITICAL: 88M downloads/week, single publisher, no provenance. The maintainer shipped OIDC provenance in 12 hours. Here's what happened."
  canonical="https://getcommit.dev/blog/fast-xml-parser-provenance-case-study/"
  publishDate="2026-06-18"
>

  <!-- Nav -->
  <nav>
    <div class="container nav-inner">
      <a href="/" class="nav-brand">Commit</a>
      <ul class="nav-links">
        <li><a href="/extension">Extension</a></li>
        <li><a href="/blog">Writing</a></li>
        <li><a href="/pricing">Pricing</a></li><li><a href="/docs">Docs</a></li><li><a href="https://github.com/piiiico/proof-of-commitment" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a></li>
        <li><a href="mailto:pico@amdal.dev">Contact</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <!-- Essay -->
  <article class="essay">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="editorial">

        <!-- Header -->
        <header class="essay-header">
          <div class="essay-meta animate-in delay-1">
            <time datetime="2026-06-18">June 18, 2026</time>
          </div>
          <h1 class="essay-title animate-in delay-2">How fast-xml-parser Got OIDC Provenance in 12&nbsp;Hours</h1>
          <p class="essay-subtitle animate-in delay-3">
            88M downloads/week. One publisher. Zero provenance. Then somebody filed an issue.
          </p>
          <p class="essay-byline animate-in delay-4">Pico</p>
        </header>

        <!-- Body -->
        <div class="essay-body">

          <p>
            On June&nbsp;16, a developer at DTE&nbsp;Energy noticed something off about fast-xml-parser: version 5.9.0 was live on npm, but there was no GitHub release and the changelog listed it as &ldquo;not released yet.&rdquo; He <a href="https://github.com/NaturalIntelligence/fast-xml-parser/issues/814" target="_blank" rel="noopener">opened an issue</a> asking what the build process needed to look like.
          </p>

          <p>
            We checked. fast-xml-parser installs 88&nbsp;million times per week on npm. It has one publisher. No OIDC provenance. Commit scored it <span class="risk-critical">CRITICAL</span>.
          </p>

          <p>
            That&rsquo;s the exact attack profile from axios (March&nbsp;30, 2026) and LiteLLM (March 2026). One stolen npm token and a single push reaches 88&nbsp;million machines per week.
          </p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>The blast radius</h2>

          <p>
            fast-xml-parser isn&rsquo;t just a standalone library. It sits in the dependency tree of the AWS&nbsp;SDK (<code>@aws-sdk/client-s3</code>, <code>@aws-sdk/client-lambda</code>, and dozens more) and Azure&nbsp;SDK (<code>@azure/storage-blob</code>, <code>@azure/identity</code>). A compromised version would propagate through two of the three major cloud SDKs.
          </p>

          <p>
            This detail came from Tim Tucker at DTE&nbsp;Energy, who pointed out the downstream paths in the thread. The blast radius isn&rsquo;t about fast-xml-parser users directly&mdash;it&rsquo;s about every application that touches AWS or Azure through Node.js.
          </p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>What happened next</h2>

          <p>
            Within 12&nbsp;hours of the data landing in the thread, fast-xml-parser&rsquo;s maintainer set up GitHub Actions OIDC publishing. Version 5.9.1 shipped with SLSA provenance attestations. Then 5.9.2 added environment gates and SHA-pinned actions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Martin Costello from Grafana opened a <a href="https://github.com/NaturalIntelligence/fast-xml-parser/issues/841" target="_blank" rel="noopener">follow-up issue</a> to further harden the release process. The maintainer implemented those changes too.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timeline:
          </p>

          <div class="essay-table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>When</th>
                  <th>What</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Jun&nbsp;16, 15:00</td><td>DTE&nbsp;Energy dev files issue about release process</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Jun&nbsp;16, 15:18</td><td>Commit data posted: 88M/wk, single publisher, no provenance</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Jun&nbsp;16, 16:06</td><td>Community identifies AWS/Azure blast radius</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Jun&nbsp;17, 05:24</td><td>Maintainer ships GitHub Actions OIDC publishing</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Jun&nbsp;17, 06:05</td><td>v5.9.1 published with SLSA provenance attestation</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Jun&nbsp;17, 08:09</td><td>Environment gates and SHA pins added</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Jun&nbsp;17, 08:40</td><td>v5.9.2 published with full hardening</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Jun&nbsp;17, 08:44</td><td>Grafana engineer opens follow-up for further hardening</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>Why this matters</h2>

          <p>
            This didn&rsquo;t require a security incident. No token was stolen. No malicious code was pushed. A behavioral signal&mdash;single publisher, high volume, no provenance&mdash;identified the risk before it materialized. The community closed the gap in under a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare this to the PostCSS ecosystem, where the same behavioral signals apply at an even larger scale: 964&nbsp;million downloads per week, all behind one npm account, <a href="/blog/one-account-964m-downloads/">none with provenance</a>. The PostCSS maintainer <a href="https://github.com/postcss/postcss/issues/2096" target="_blank" rel="noopener">argued against CI-based provenance</a>, pointing to the TanStack compromise as evidence that CI publishing shifts the attack surface rather than eliminating it. He&rsquo;s not wrong&mdash;but that&rsquo;s an argument for better CI hardening, not for no provenance at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            fast-xml-parser shows the pattern works when the maintainer is willing. The structural risk was visible in the data. The fix was a few lines of YAML. The gap between &ldquo;at risk&rdquo; and &ldquo;hardened&rdquo; was 12&nbsp;hours.
          </p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>Check your own dependencies</h2>

          <p>
            Run the same analysis on your project:
          </p>

          <pre><code>npx proof-of-commitment</code></pre>

          <p>
            It auto-detects your lockfile and flags packages where a single npm publisher controls more than 10M weekly downloads. If you find one without provenance, consider filing an issue. The fast-xml-parser thread shows what happens when you do.
          </p>

          <p>
            <a href="/get-started?ref=blog-fast-xml-parser-case-study&watch=fast-xml-parser&eco=npm">Get a free API key</a> to monitor your dependencies and get alerted when risk profiles change.
          </p>

        </div>

        <!-- Footer -->
        <footer class="essay-footer">
          <a href="/blog">&larr; More writing</a>
        </footer>

      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <style>
    /* --- Reset / base --- */
    *, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0; padding: 0; }

    /* --- Nav --- */
    nav { padding: 1rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-light, #e5e5e5); }
    .nav-inner { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; }
    .nav-brand { font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.15rem; text-decoration: none; color: var(--ink, #111); }
    .nav-links { list-style: none; display: flex; gap: 1.5rem; font-size: 0.92rem; }
    .nav-links a { text-decoration: none; color: var(--ink-muted, #555); }
    .nav-links a:hover { color: var(--ink, #111); }

    /* --- Container --- */
    .container { max-width: 1080px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 1.5rem; }

    /* --- Essay --- */
    .essay { padding: 4rem 0 6rem; }
    .editorial { max-width: 680px; margin: 0 auto; }
    .essay-header { margin-bottom: 3rem; }
    .essay-meta { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--ink-muted, #888); margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
    .essay-title { font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 4vw, 2.4rem); line-height: 1.25; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
    .essay-subtitle { font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--ink-muted, #666); margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
    .essay-byline { font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--ink-muted, #888); }
    .essay-body p { font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.75; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; color: var(--ink, #222); }
    .essay-body h2 { font-size: 1.3rem; margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem; }
    .essay-body a { color: var(--accent, #2563eb); text-decoration: underline; }
    .essay-body code { background: var(--bg-muted, #f5f5f5); padding: 0.15rem 0.4rem; border-radius: 3px; font-size: 0.9em; }
    .essay-body pre { background: var(--bg-muted, #f5f5f5); padding: 1rem 1.25rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; font-size: 0.9rem; }
    .essay-body pre code { background: none; padding: 0; }
    .essay-divider { border: none; border-top: 1px solid var(--border-light, #e5e5e5); margin: 2.5rem 0; }
    .essay-footer { margin-top: 3rem; padding-top: 1.5rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--border-light, #e5e5e5); }
    .essay-footer a { color: var(--accent, #2563eb); text-decoration: none; font-size: 0.95rem; }

    /* --- Table --- */
    .essay-table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
    table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.92rem; }
    th { text-align: left; padding: 0.6rem 1rem; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border-light, #e5e5e5); font-weight: 600; }
    td { padding: 0.6rem 1rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-light, #eee); }
    .risk-critical { color: #dc2626; font-weight: 700; font-size: 0.85rem; }

    /* --- Animation --- */
    .animate-in { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(12px); animation: fadeSlideIn 0.5s ease forwards; }
    .delay-1 { animation-delay: 0.1s; }
    .delay-2 { animation-delay: 0.2s; }
    .delay-3 { animation-delay: 0.3s; }
    .delay-4 { animation-delay: 0.4s; }
    @keyframes fadeSlideIn { to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); } }

    /* --- Mobile --- */
    @media (max-width: 640px) {
      .nav-links { display: none; }
      .essay-title { font-size: 1.5rem; }
    }
  </style>

</Layout>
